{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: forecasts are generally below realized prices, especially during sustained uptrends. Apply an upward level shift (≈ +1 to +3 €/t) when the last 10–15 days show a rising regime (higher highs / higher lows).",
    "Trend underreaction / lag: after upside breaks (multi-day acceleration), the model follows too slowly and misses the magnitude for ~1–2 weeks. Increase slope/tilt for days 5–20 of the horizon when recent 5–10d momentum is strongly positive.",
    "Late-horizon downside overshoot: in several cases the model projects a sharp selloff in the last third of the horizon that does not materialize (or is much milder). Damp negative drift for days ~20–30; cap cumulative drawdown unless recent history contains an actual breakdown.",
    "Volatility underestimation around rallies: realized paths show larger upside swings than forecast during bullish phases. Widen the upper tail / add convexity (higher highs) when volatility is rising alongside positive momentum.",
    "Mean-reversion miscalibration: the model reverts toward lower levels too aggressively after prices rise. Reduce mean-reversion strength when the 30d history ends near highs and fundamentals/regime appear trend-following (i.e., avoid forcing reversion within 30d)."
  ]
}
